# A `where`-bound lambda whose parameter annotation contradicts the use site.
#
# Before lambda parameter annotations were honoured this compiled clean: the
# parameter's type was solved from `wrap_it(n)` and the written `String` was
# never consulted, so the annotation was decoration permitted to lie.
#
# The error lands on the CALL, not on the annotation, and that is the point. A
# `where` binding has no callee slot, so the lambda is inferred first and the
# annotation simply installs `String`; `wrap_it(n)` is then checked against the
# signature the author wrote. An explicit annotation is believed and the use is
# the mistake. Argument position reports the other way round — the slot is known
# before the lambda is inferred — see lambda_annotation_mismatch_arg.
module main

fn shout(n: Int) -> String =
  wrap_it(n)
where
  wrap_it = \ (s: String) -> `<${s}>`

fn main() -> Unit !{IO} = print(shout(3))
